WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.276
Theorem 3.4.13. If f has continuous partial derivatives

2
00:00:03.276 --> 00:00:06.245
and a nonzero gradient at a, b,

3
00:00:06.245 --> 00:00:09.828
then the gradient is perpendicular to the level curve

4
00:00:09.828 --> 00:00:11.875
through a, b. Here's why.

5
00:00:12.275 --> 00:00:15.727
Continuous partial derivatives make f differentiable at a, b.

6
00:00:15.727 --> 00:00:19.563
Let c equal f of a, b. By Theorem 3.4.11,

7
00:00:19.563 --> 00:00:24.550
the tangent plane to the graph at a, b, c has this equation.

8
00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:29.550
Along the level curve f of x, y equals c, the height is constant,

9
00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:33.355
so the lifted curve lies in the horizontal plane z equals c.

10
00:00:33.355 --> 00:00:36.976
If v is a tangent direction to the level curve,

11
00:00:36.976 --> 00:00:40.650
the lifted curve has tangent direction v one, v two, zero.

12
00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:44.770
Its tangent line, r of t, passes through a, b, c,

13
00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.470
with direction vector v one, v two, zero,

14
00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:49.525
and it lies in the tangent plane.

15
00:00:49.925 --> 00:00:56.799
So x equals a plus t v one, y equals b plus t v two, and z equals c

16
00:00:56.799 --> 00:00:58.246
satisfy the plane equation.

17
00:00:58.246 --> 00:01:02.225
Substituting and subtracting c leaves zero equals t times this sum.

18
00:01:02.625 --> 00:01:05.081
For t not zero, divide by t.

19
00:01:05.081 --> 00:01:08.610
The gradient of f at a, b, dot v, equals zero.

20
00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:12.800
So the gradient is perpendicular to the level curve's tangent direction.
